<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Good Judgment, Bad Advice: Decision-Model Controllers
<br class="ltx_break">Raise Agent Cost Without Improving Success</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Naman Vats
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">be10360.17@bitmesra.ac.in</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Agent scaffolds increasingly delegate small decisions (whether to stop, verify, or change approach) to cheaper models.
We ask whether a typed decision model, Jev 1.13, consulted only at deterministic decision points, makes a Claude Sonnet 5.5 terminal agent cheaper without making it worse.
In a pre-specified study on Terminal-Bench 2.1, we hold one agent loop fixed and vary only the controller: none, deterministic rules, a small LLM (Claude Haiku 5.5) answering the same questions, Jev, and a placebo that samples Jev’s development-phase choice frequencies at the same moments.
On 21 held-out tasks (315 trials), Jev raised cost per attempt by 19% (geometric-mean ratio 1.19, 95% CI 1.02–1.36) at an identical pass rate (73.0% vs. 73.0%).
Jev was cheaper than the small-LLM controller (ratio 0.81) but 22% more expensive than the placebo, and no pass-rate difference between arms was detectable.
Offline, Jev ranked finish requests better than the alternatives (AUROC 0.78 vs. 0.62 for rules and 0.60 for the small LLM) but was poorly calibrated: its mean probability that a run was complete was 0.50 when 87% of those runs passed, so it asked the agent to verify work that was already correct.
No controller ever chose to abandon a run.
Informative decision signals did not become useful interventions; we discuss what that would require.
Code: <a href="https://github.com/namanvats/decision-model-controllers" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/namanvats/decision-model-controllers</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Terminal agents solve tasks by running long loops of model calls and shell commands <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Their cost is dominated by the reasoning model, and scaffolds add control logic around it: when to stop, when to check work, when to change approach.
A natural way to make that logic both smarter and cheaper is to delegate it to a small model.
Typed decision models are built for this: given a state and a fixed set of options, they return a choice and a probability distribution, at a small fraction of the price of a reasoning model.
Jev 1.13 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">TypeSafe, 2026</a>)</cite>, for example, is priced at $0.042 per million input tokens; in our runs a decision cost about $0.00008.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A controller can only save money if its judgments carry information about how the run will end <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">and</em> its actions turn that information into savings.
Evaluations of judgment models mostly measure the first <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kadavath et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
We test both, end to end, with the baselines that separate them: deterministic rules (is a model needed at all?), a small LLM asked the same questions (why not a cheap LLM?), and a placebo that intervenes at the same moments with random choices drawn from Jev’s own choice frequencies (does the decision content matter, or only the nudge?).
Our contributions are a pre-specified, placebo-controlled design that varies only the controller of a fixed agent loop (<a href="#S3" title="3 Study design ‣ Good Judgment, Bad Advice: Decision-Model ControllersRaise Agent Cost Without Improving Success" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>); a confirmatory result that Jev raised cost by 19% without changing the pass rate (<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Confirmatory: Jev versus no controller ‣ 4 Results ‣ Good Judgment, Bad Advice: Decision-Model ControllersRaise Agent Cost Without Improving Success" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>); comparisons with the small LLM, rules and placebo (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Secondary: small LLM, rules and placebo ‣ 4 Results ‣ Good Judgment, Bad Advice: Decision-Model ControllersRaise Agent Cost Without Improving Success" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>); and an account of why, from offline judgment quality and the logged decisions (<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Offline decision quality ‣ 4 Results ‣ Good Judgment, Bad Advice: Decision-Model ControllersRaise Agent Cost Without Improving Success" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sections</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Where the extra cost comes from ‣ 4 Results ‣ Good Judgment, Bad Advice: Decision-Model ControllersRaise Agent Cost Without Improving Success" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ReAct-style loops interleave reasoning with tool calls <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and the interface around the model matters as much as the model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Reflexion adds verbal self-feedback between attempts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; by contrast, asking a model to re-check its work without new external information often fails to improve it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; our verification nudge is of this kind, although the agent can gather new evidence by running commands.
Cascades and routers send queries to cheaper models when possible <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; we study a different lever, where the reasoning model is fixed and a cheap model decides only how the loop proceeds.
Selective prediction and calibration study when confidence can be acted on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geifman and El-Yaniv, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, LLMs can estimate whether their own answers are correct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kadavath et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and LLM judges are widely used to grade outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
We measure ranking and calibration offline, and then whether acting on them helps online.</p>
</div>
</section>
