<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Offline decision quality</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To separate judgment from action, we replayed every trigger state logged by native in the evaluation to each controller, without acting on the answers, and scored them against the trial’s grader outcome (<a href="#S4.T3" title="In 4.3 Offline decision quality ‣ 4 Results ‣ Good Judgment, Bad Advice: Decision-Model ControllersRaise Agent Cost Without Improving Success" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
For Q-finish the score is the probability of <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">finish</span> and the label is a pass; for Q-progress it is the probability of <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abandon</span> and the label is a failure; rules and the small LLM return a single choice, scored as 1 or 0.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Jev ranked finish requests best (AUROC 0.78, against 0.62 for rules and 0.60 for the small LLM), but it was miscalibrated toward “not done”: its mean probability of <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">finish</span> was 0.50, while 87% of these runs passed.
It therefore chose <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">verify</span> for 29 of 53 finish requests, and its argmax accuracy (0.55) fell below the base rate (0.87).
The ranking is real: runs given <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\texttt{finish})\geq 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">finish</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\texttt{finish})\geq 0.5</annotation></semantics></math> passed 96% of the time (23 of 24) and runs below 0.5 passed 79% (23 of 29), so even the runs Jev doubted most were mostly correct.
On Q-progress no controller ever chose <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abandon</span>, and Jev’s probability of abandoning barely separated failing from passing runs (AUROC 0.55).
The 111 states logged during calibration showed the same pattern (finish AUROC 0.77 for Jev and 0.46 for the small LLM), although they predate the final verification feature.</p>
</div>
