<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Where the extra cost comes from</h3>

<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Verification and longer runs.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cost rose with how often a controller asked the agent to verify (<a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4 Results ‣ Good Judgment, Bad Advice: Decision-Model ControllersRaise Agent Cost Without Improving Success" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>): placebo 0.33 verification messages per attempt (cost ratio 0.98), Jev 0.51 (1.19), rules 0.92 (1.24) and small LLM 1.30 (1.47).
Verification rounds alone do not account for Jev’s increase: of its extra $0.029 per attempt, $0.011 was spent after the first verification message.
The rest came from longer trajectories throughout (16.0 steps per attempt against 13.3), consistent with its progress messages (Jev chose <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">investigate</span> or <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">recover</span> at 30 of 92 progress decisions), although this part of the decomposition is correlational.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Spread across tasks.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Jev was more expensive on 15 of 21 tasks (<a href="#S4.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 Secondary: small LLM, rules and placebo ‣ 4 Results ‣ Good Judgment, Bad Advice: Decision-Model ControllersRaise Agent Cost Without Improving Success" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a>), and removing any single task leaves the ratio between 1.16 and 1.25.
Of the six cheaper tasks, three were within 3% of native; the three clear reductions were hard tasks whose runs mostly ended at Harbor’s timeout or the budget cap, where there is usually no finish request and therefore nothing to verify.
Pass rates differed between Jev and native on only three tasks (<a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Per-task results ‣ Good Judgment, Bad Advice: Decision-Model ControllersRaise Agent Cost Without Improving Success" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>); easy tasks passed almost always and hard tasks almost never.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Abandon was never used.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">No controller chose <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abandon</span> in any of the 315 trials, so G2 never had to block one; the placebo could not abandon by construction.
The only action designed to save money played no part, and hopeless runs ran until a limit: all 15 runs of <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">make-doom-for-mips</span> failed at Harbor’s timeout (11) or the $1.00 cap (4).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Overhead.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Controller calls were negligible: Jev averaged 0.52 s per decision and 0.11% of spend, the small LLM 1.46 s and 0.47%; no call timed out or returned a malformed answer.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why informed control did not pay.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Three properties of this setting work against an advisory controller.
First, the agent’s own finish requests were usually right (87% of native finish requests passed), so a verification round is mostly spent re-checking correct work.
Second, Jev’s probabilities rank well but sit far below the base rate, so acting on its argmax converts a good ranking into frequent, low-value interventions; a base-rate-aware threshold would intervene far less, but even Jev’s least confident group passed 79% of the time.
Third, most failures (10 of 17 for native) ended at a timeout or a cap rather than with a finish request, and no controller proposed to abandon them; the abandon guard was never the obstacle.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">What the placebo shows.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Random guidance at the same moments matched every informed controller’s pass rate without their cost.
Without the placebo, the comparison against native alone would suggest only that Jev is somewhat expensive; with it, the evidence says that Jev’s choices, not the act of intervening, add the cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implications.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Controllers for agent loops should be evaluated end to end on cost, against rules and a placebo, not only on judgment accuracy.
Typed decision models are cheap and fast enough that overhead is not the problem (Jev cost 19% less than the small-LLM controller and answered about three times faster); what matters is the policy that maps their probabilities to actions.
At scale, a 19% increase in cost per attempt applies to every task an agent runs.
A follow-up should act on calibrated probabilities with base-rate-aware thresholds and give the controller stopping actions it will actually use, pre-specified on new tasks.
Two lessons for evaluation harnesses also emerged: a naive “did the agent verify?” feature misses agents that check work by running their own programs, and safety refusals return an empty reply that a loop can mistake for a finish request.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sample.</em> The held-out set has 21 tasks, 14 of them easy and only 3 mid, so pass-rate intervals are wide (about <math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>11–13 pp) and the C1 guardrail is near the design’s resolution. The cost result is robust (15 of 21 tasks; leave-one-out 1.16–1.25), but the pass-rate result is weak evidence either way.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">One agent and one decision model.</em> We study one reasoning model in one minimal loop, with Jev 1.13 and our option wording and messages. A pre-specified replication on an open-weight model was not run: its OpenRouter hosts serve different quantizations, so the model could not be held fixed across arms.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Nondeterminism and hosts.</em> Sonnet 5.5 accepts no temperature parameter and hosts were not pinned; attempts are replicates, and the host mix was similar across arms.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Placebo.</em> Its distribution comes from a small development sample (8 progress and 23 finish decisions) and gives abandon zero probability; it tests decision content, not intervention frequency.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Strata and labels.</em> Strata come from two calibration attempts, and offline labels use the trial outcome rather than the counterfactual outcome of a different decision.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A cheap, fast typed decision model consulted at well-defined decision points did not make a strong terminal agent cheaper: it raised cost by 19% at an unchanged pass rate, and a placebo matched its pass rate for less.
Its judgments were informative but miscalibrated, its interventions mostly re-checked correct work, and the one cost-saving action it could take was never chosen.
The gap between an informative signal and a useful intervention is the policy in between, and that policy should be tested with placebo controls and cost as the primary outcome.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reproducibility.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The benchmark version, Harbor version, model identifiers, caps, trigger and guard parameters, and all prompts are given in the paper and its appendices; every trial records its configuration fingerprint, lockfile hash and Harbor command.
The harness, configurations and analysis scripts are publicly available.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
